My mother smiles on the screen of my mobile phone.
"I have never heard your story," she says.
"What did you do that morning at the airport?"
I think back to the day in January.
"I waited at the counter," I answer.
"I counted the people in the queue."
My mother asks, "Why did you do that?"
"That calmed me down," I explain to her.
Then I tell her a little secret.
"I have not told anyone that yet," I begin.
"But I cried at the gate."
My mother looks at me very affectionately.
"I thought so," she says quietly.
"I looked through the glass," I continue.
"And I watched you for a very long time."
"We looked for you too," she says.
I did not know that back then.
I hold the mobile phone tightly and smile.
